					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/petrograd" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(455145); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=455145&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1606380139" alt="Petrograd" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=455145)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/petrograd" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(455145); return false;" >Petrograd</a></span> <span class="odate time_1537160600 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">17 Sep 2018 05:03</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-3973160">
									

<p>-1. Ok, some basic feedback, less than what I'd probably give in a draft forum.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>BIG PICTURE PROBLEM, this doesn't feel like a fully developed idea. &quot;Big underground multi-limb reptiles&quot; is kind of an interesting mental image, but not enough to fully carry this. The biggest trait about this thing is that it's dangerous, potentially catastrophic, but danger isn't very interesting. Tying it to other real eruptions and implying it could erupt soon/faster are both part of that &quot;woo danger&quot; thing, so they don't add much. You spend a lot of time spelling out the past breaches, but you don't go beyond just mentioning the narrative elements of past eruptions: What happened when they erupted in the past (except that it kills things)? Why do they erupt? Why do they follow a schedule? There could be interesting answers behind those questions, but you don't address any of them, or you noncommittally mention theories. Why should I <em>care</em> about past or future eruptions? &quot;It could destroy human society&quot; is a little interesting the way you describe it, but right now that's just more danger and it's in a sea of bland.</p>
<p>The reveal that there are many in other volcanos across the world was actually really cool, but it didn't address the gap in the equation.</p>
<p>ON TO SPECIFICS:<br />
Your containment procedures are way too long and full of uninteresting detail. Compared to similar SCPs, you've got more than twice as much as the (similarly dangerous) <a href="/scp-169">SCP-169</a>. Less than <a href="/scp-1179">SCP-1179</a>, but that one uses the containment procedures as a storytelling device, yours doesn't. Figure out how many times you're saying the same thing different ways (we can't contain this, there's going to be a disaster, etc.), pick the best one or two ways, and move the hell on.</p>
<p>The Greek letters paragraph is kind of pointless. I'm ok with jargon, but why designate these things? And I don't mean why in-universe, I mean <em>why are you taking my time as a reader to insert a letter, explain that letter, and then use it in a SINGLE sentence</em>? If you have a good answer for that, great, write to fit that. If not, why is it there?</p>
<p>Also, there's no real build-up. Your containment starts with &quot;it's gonna blow up society!&quot;, where can you go from there?</p>
<p>Probably my first major issue was with the description, where you're just kind of spelling out what they &quot;believe&quot; this thing looks like. It's like&#8230; you're giving too much detail for the situation of vagueness you've already set up. I feel like not only are you doing a lot of plain exposition (telling not showing) throughout the article, it's <em>speculative</em> plain exposition, which is somehow worse. Especially for the description, just write it as if they somehow know this stuff, or go with &quot;we can't detect much&quot; and don't go into the detail.</p>
<p>Split your sentences up. I don't think they're technically run-on, but you've got like 4-5 clauses in some of these.</p>
<p>Probably the second big problem, writing wise:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Between giant lizards, evil staircases, and god knows what else running around on the surface the thought of yet another thing to worry about seemed far from favorable.<br />
You know the mantra, “we die in the dark, so the rest of humanity can live in the light,&quot;</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Referencing the most popular articles for flavor alone gives the impression that you've read about 5 SCPs total and wanted to reference them all. Whether or not that's true, it drastically undercuts people's ability to take this seriously.<br />
1032 and 2000 were more relevant, but 1032 feels a bit random when there aren't any other crosslinks and the 2000 sentence <em>definitely</em> didn't pack enough of a punch to deserve its own level 5 collapsible. (And why be cagey about the link in a level 5 collapsible?) It would be much better if integrated into the procedures or the previous collapsible.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

